{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793575542,"nonce":"MXs1oNOiiF0d4CsFdSM3JbONNLU=","message":"Implemented in the commit above (not the AIR lifter): $asm functions are compiled before their first call by the LLVM tier on x86-64 hosts. A run of Asm ops becomes one inline-asm block (bytes as the template, modes 1-3 as pinned in/out operands loaded from and stored to the VM registers, which AIR now pins to cells), refused when modes interleave, for naked functions (mode 4), registers 4/5, and non-x86-64 targets. Cranelift declines; --mode jit and hybrid fall back to LLVM; the interpreter and other hosts raise an error naming the function instead of skipping the ops.\nCheck, NUC x86-64 release build: fixtures made with crates/ash/examples/asm_fixture.rs rewrite add(a, b) into eax/ecx loads, an add / sub / imul byte sequence and an eax store. Stock hl 1.15 and Ash (hybrid, jit, jit --jit-tier llvm) print identical results for all three (add: 5 42 and sum -1474336480; sub: -1 38; imul: 6 80). Interp fails with the new error. Test: crates/ash_cli/tests/asm_opcode.rs with asm_add.hl and asm_imul.hl.\nFound on the way: Haxe 4.3.6 has no $asm; only the development build does (genhl.ml). Open: naked functions, XMM operands and 64-bit registers are lowered but untested, an AOT build of an asm function is untested, and the AIR lifter for non-x86 hosts.","files":null}]}